A window under an `if` that reads state, on the two targets that show one
window: the `Screen` it is composed to is an ordinary node in a view both
re-render whole, so it is on screen while the condition holds and gone when it
turns false, and nothing in it is kept. Its `@closed` writes `details`, so the
close takes the window out of the tree, which leaves nothing on screen and
ends the program.

`n` is the window's own state: a counter written in its body is the body's,
hoisted to the package, and starts from zero only once.

deny * `unexpected nested Window` -- the window is a Screen node, not the builtin

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

var (
    details = true
    n = 0
)

if details {
    ui.window(title="Load", @closed {
        details = false
    }) {
        ui.text(value="{n}")
        ui.button(text="+", @click {
            n += 1
        })
    }
}
